                'excerpt' => 'Step-by-step guide to creating a valid Power of Attorney in India from abroad — apostille, notarisation, and registration.',
                'category'=> 'Legal',
                'content' => '<p>A Power of Attorney (POA) allows you to authorise a trusted person in India to act on your behalf for property, financial, or legal matters.</p><h2>Types of POA</h2><ul><li><strong>General POA:</strong> Broad authority over multiple matters</li><li><strong>Special POA:</strong> Limited to specific transactions</li></ul><h2>Steps to Create POA from Abroad</h2><ol><li>Draft the POA document with specific powers listed clearly</li><li>Sign before an Indian Consulate/Embassy in your country</li><li>Get it apostilled by the relevant authority</li><li>Send to India for registration at Sub-Registrar office</li></ol><h2>Important Notes</h2><p>The POA must be registered in India for property transactions. Ensure it is notarised and apostilled in your country of residence before sending to India.</p>',
