                'excerpt' => 'The PIO card scheme was discontinued in 2015. Here is what OCI card holders need to know about their rights and benefits.',
                'category'=> 'Immigration',
                'content' => '<p>The PIO (Person of Indian Origin) card scheme was discontinued in January 2015, and all PIO cardholders were advised to convert to OCI cards.</p><h2>OCI Card Benefits</h2><ul><li>Lifelong multiple-entry visa to India</li><li>No registration with FRRO for any length of stay</li><li>Parity with NRIs for economic, financial, and educational matters</li><li>Cannot hold public office or vote</li></ul><h2>PIO to OCI Conversion</h2><p>If you still hold a PIO card, you should convert it to an OCI card. The process is similar to a fresh OCI application and requires submission of your PIO card along with standard documents.</p><h2>OCI vs NRI</h2><p>An NRI (Non-Resident Indian) is an Indian citizen living abroad. An OCI is a foreign national of Indian origin. Both have significant rights in India, but OCI holders cannot own agricultural land.</p>',
